#{[17592186399183 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399183 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399183 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e287a52-db21-559f-9d89-5103b7019f80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399184 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399184 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399184 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f5969ae4-0089-56bd-bf20-0cf41f4333df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399185 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399185 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399185 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cd0d464b-7ff4-56b8-bcb7-57b51b72d32d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399186 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399186 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db4a7149-d824-54c0-86ee-b21c01fb5cf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401579 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401579 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401579 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401579 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7d00266-1ded-5f4c-819a-7467528496c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401579 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2x,\\,2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=0 \\\\ 2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186401582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401580 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc59060d-a9ed-5533-b31b-311227554f8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401581 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401581 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401581 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7cd4c18b-e511-5b14-9f13-a5b4cb4f47f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401582 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401583 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401583 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401583 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "77dac977-36a1-5c53-9361-88fca32b4abf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401584 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401584 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401584 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d746e3e5-aa5e-5fa1-88ee-69128529ad71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401585 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401585 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401585 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18156a3d-068d-5e69-8ef7-8771427933c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404350 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404350 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404350 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404350 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404350 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32f5ebff-a024-5f63-b943-142f3a504252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404350 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/96/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186404354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404351 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c1c6b9bc-2cad-5aef-8fbc-0df5f811dfbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404352 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404352 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404352 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ead01c60-0b8e-5265-95fa-6b4cdaec742d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404353 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404353 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404353 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db0835b3-9555-5f10-b783-ce2279912fb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404354 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404354 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404354 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404355 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23d9111e-7ce1-561b-9f4f-29ebc2c46ba5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404356 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404356 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404356 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31a2fcdf-48af-528a-bc62-4f070d949ae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404357 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404357 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404357 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404357 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/20/20f279f5936987374965cbba01e7e089d88dae1c62767b1ab8eb9ebaea0e5ce9.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=6x-2xy$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-{x}^{2}/2},$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404357 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25973738-10c4-5a96-a292-a5b1bfa48a02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404357 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}6-2y,\\,-2x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6-2y &=0 \\\\ -2x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,3).$ Since the point $(0,3)$ does not belong to the closed region of $D,$ we do not include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(0) \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}+2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation has no solution inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$ So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain two candidates:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at